From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Quarrier <squarrier@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 08:27:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] glm5_next/cuda: a take-over decides which kept prompts stay
 before it copies any, TF_GLM_DECIDE_THEN_COPY (MiaAI-Lab recipe patch 0078)

A request that takes the caches over (a new conversation, one stream at a time) saved the rows of every kept snapshot
the next prefill overwrites, in order, and dropped the oldest ones whenever the next copy did not fit the budget: a
fresh conversation after a long one cloned every turn's state, each larger than the last, and dropped each for the
next, so the allocator's reserved memory grew to the sum of their sizes. On two Sparks both ranks died with
NV_ERR_NO_MEMORY. MiaAI-Lab's recipe v1.8 decides first and copies after, with patch
0078-glm-take-over-decide-then-copy: which snapshots stay is worked out from their sizes alone, the rest are dropped
without a copy, and only the survivors are cloned; the snapshots that stay and the replies are the same by design.
After a 256k, 21-turn conversation, a fresh one on two Sparks: save_rows 22 -> 1, reserved +14.8 -> +1.4 GiB,
take_over 3.3 -> 0.10 s (the recipe's CHANGELOG). On the AFD split this is the attention node's (rank 0 of world 1,
which holds every kept prompt); the expert nodes keep no snapshots and never take over. This ports it behind a switch
of this tree's own, off by default:

- engine.py: decide_then_copy() reads TF_GLM_DECIDE_THEN_COPY (0 or unset: off, 1: on, anything else refused at
  start) at each take-over; _take_over runs _take_over_decided when it is on: the patch's walk, verbatim but for its
  calls passing the drafter only when there is one, as this tree's _take_over does. A startup line when it is on.
  Under --parallel the pool keeps prompts in place and _take_over is not called: the switch acts with --parallel 1
  and on the label scores' prefill.
- THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md: "GLM take-over, decided then copied".

Unset, _take_over walks as before.

Tests (CPU): tests/test_glm_decide_then_copy.py (8): 400 random kept caches (snapshots of the live conversation and of
others, some already saved, with and without DFlash2's window, a resume point or none) taken over both ways keep,
save and drop the same snapshots with the same bytes and windows, and empty torch's pool after the same drops; the
decided walk copies only the snapshots that stay, never more bytes, and never holds more than the budget; the
recipe's case, 22 turns of one conversation then a fresh one: 22 copies before, 1 after, the same one kept; a
mutation of any of its three rules fails the random cases. tests/test_glm_cuda_kept.py's three tests run with the
switch unset and set (its stand-in snapshot gains decode.Snapshot's drafter_end and drafter_rows, which the decided
walk reads), and so do tests/test_glm_score_cache.py's three, a decision's take-over (its stand-in snapshot gains
decode.Snapshot's drafter_rows).

